{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Diffusion-Controlled Chronoamperometry with Soft Potato\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook models a diffusion-controlled potential-step experiment at a planar macroelectrode using Soft Potato. We will:\n",
    "\n",
    "- formulate the diffusion problem from electrochemistry;\n",
    "- explain the boundary conditions and the PDE physically;\n",
    "- simulate one chronoamperogram with a numerical solver;\n",
    "- compare the numerical current with the analytical Cotterell/Cottrell equation;\n",
    "- compare the implemented solvers in terms of accuracy, stability, and runtime.\n",
    "\n",
    "We use the following electrochemical reduction:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "O + ne^- \\rightarrow R\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "For a sufficiently negative potential step, the reaction is diffusion-controlled and the concentration field evolves according to Fick's second law:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\frac{\\partial C}{\\partial t} = D \\frac{\\partial^2 C}{\\partial x^2}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "with the boundary conditions\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "C(0,t)=0, \\qquad C(\\infty,t)=C^*\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where:\n",
    "\n",
    "- $C(0,t)=0$ represents consumption of the reactant at the electrode surface;\n",
    "- $C(\\infty,t)=C^*$ represents the bulk concentration far from the electrode.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import time\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "from scipy.special import erf\n",
    "\n",
    "import softpotato as sp\n",
    "from softpotato.solver import (\n",
    "    DiffusionProblem,\n",
    "    DirichletBC,\n",
    "    get_solver,\n",
    "    list_solvers,\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Soft Potato version: {sp.__version__}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Available solvers: {list_solvers()}\")\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. Physical interpretation of the diffusion problem\n",
    "\n",
    "The electrode is an absorbing boundary. Species are consumed immediately at the surface, so the concentration there is set to zero:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "C(0,t) = 0\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The far boundary is a reservoir of bulk solution:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "C(L,t) = C^*\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The initial condition is uniform bulk concentration:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "C(x,0)=C^*\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The concentration gradient at the electrode controls the flux. For a diffusion-controlled experiment the current is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "I(t)=nFAJ(t)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "with\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "J(t) = -D\\left.\\frac{\\partial C}{\\partial x}\\right|_{x=0}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "For the semi-infinite analytical solution, this yields the Cotterell/Cottrell equation:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "I(t)=nFAC^*\\sqrt{\\frac{D}{\\pi t}}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This is the signature of diffusion-controlled chronoamperometry.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Electrochemical parameters\n",
    "D = 1.0e-5          # diffusion coefficient, cm^2/s\n",
    "C_bulk = 1.0e-6     # bulk concentration, mol/cm^3\n",
    "n = 1               # electrons transferred\n",
    "F = 96485.33212     # C/mol\n",
    "A = 0.0707          # electrode area, cm^2\n",
    "\n",
    "# Spatial domain approximating an infinite solution\n",
    "L = 0.10\n",
    "Nx = 201\n",
    "x = np.linspace(0.0, L, Nx)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Time domain\n",
    "t_end = 5.0\n",
    "t_eval = np.linspace(0.0, t_end, 251)\n",
    "\n",
    "problem = DiffusionProblem(\n",
    "    grid=x,\n",
    "    diffusivity={\"O\": D},\n",
    "    initial_conditions={\"O\": C_bulk},\n",
    "    boundary_conditions={\n",
    "        \"O\": (\n",
    "            DirichletBC(0.0),\n",
    "            DirichletBC(C_bulk),\n",
    "        )\n",
    "    },\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"dx = {problem.dx:.3e} cm\")\n",
    "print(f\"Diffusion length at t_end: {np.sqrt(D*t_end):.3e} cm\")\n",
    "print(f\"L / sqrt(D*t_end) = {L / np.sqrt(D*t_end):.2f}\")\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. Analytical functions\n",
    "\n",
    "The analytical concentration profile for semi-infinite diffusion is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "C(x,t)=C^*\\,\\mathrm{erf}\\left(\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{Dt}}\\right)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The mass-transfer coefficient is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "k_m(t)=\\sqrt{\\frac{D}{\\pi t}}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The current is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "I(t)=nFAk_m(t)C^*\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "and therefore:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "I(t)=nFAC^*\\sqrt{\\frac{D}{\\pi t}}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "The diffusion mass-transfer coefficient is time dependent, which is exactly the behaviour seen in chronoamperometry.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def analytical_concentration(x, t, D, C_bulk):\n    \"\"\"Semi-infinite concentration profile.\"\"\"\n    return C_bulk * erf(x / (2.0 * np.sqrt(D * t)))\n",
    "\n",
    "def analytical_flux(t, D, C_bulk):\n    \"\"\"Positive magnitude of the flux arriving at the electrode.\"\"\"\n    return C_bulk * np.sqrt(D / (np.pi * t))\n",
    "\n",
    "def analytical_current(t, n, F, A, D, C_bulk):\n    \"\"\"Cotterell/Cottrell current.\"\"\"\n    return n * F * A * analytical_flux(t, D, C_bulk)\n",
    "\n",
    "def analytical_km(t, D):\n    \"\"\"Time-dependent mass-transfer coefficient.\"\"\"\n    return np.sqrt(D / (np.pi * t))\n",
    "\n",
    "positive_times = t_eval > 0.0\n",
    "I_exact_ref = analytical_current(\n",
    "    t_eval[positive_times],\n",
    "    n=n,\n",
    "    F=F,\n",
    "    A=A,\n",
    "    D=D,\n",
    "    C_bulk=C_bulk,\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Analytical current at t=1 s: {I_exact_ref[50]:.3e} A\")\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. Solve one chronoamperogram with one solver\n",
    "\n",
    "We first simulate one chronoamperogram using the adaptive `scipy_ivp` solver. This is a useful starting point because it gives a clean, physically interpretable result before we compare the numerical solvers.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "solver = get_solver(\n",
    "    \"scipy_ivp\",\n",
    "    method=\"Radau\",\n",
    "    rtol=1e-7,\n",
    "    atol=1e-10,\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "start = time.perf_counter()\n",
    "result = solver.solve(\n",
    "    problem,\n",
    "    t_span=(0.0, t_end),\n",
    "    t_eval=t_eval,\n",
    ")\n",
    "elapsed = time.perf_counter() - start\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Success: {result.success}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Message: {result.message}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Wall-clock time: {elapsed:.3f} s\")\n",
    "\n",
    "flux = result.fluxes[\"O\"]\n",
    "current = n * F * A * flux\n",
    "positive = result.t > 0.0\n",
    "current_exact = analytical_current(\n",
    "    result.t[positive],\n",
    "    n=n,\n",
    "    F=F,\n",
    "    A=A,\n",
    "    D=D,\n",
    "    C_bulk=C_bulk,\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(12, 4))\n",
    "\n",
    "ax[0].plot(\n",
    "    result.t[positive],\n",
    "    current[positive] * 1e6,\n",
    "    label=\"Soft Potato (Radau)\",\n",
    ")\n",
    "ax[0].plot(\n",
    "    result.t[positive],\n",
    "    current_exact * 1e6,\n",
    "    \"k--\",\n",
    "    lw=2,\n",
    "    label=\"Cotterell analytical\",\n",
    ")\n",
    "ax[0].set_xlabel(\"Time / s\")\n",
    "ax[0].set_ylabel(\"Current / µA\")\n",
    "ax[0].set_title(\"Chronoamperogram\")\n",
    "ax[0].grid(alpha=0.3)\n",
    "ax[0].legend()\n",
    "\n",
    "final_index = -1\n",
    "profile = result[\"O\"][final_index]\n",
    "ax[1].plot(x, profile, label=f\"t = {result.t[final_index]:.2f} s\")\n",
    "ax[1].plot(\n",
    "    x,\n",
    "    analytical_concentration(x, result.t[final_index], D, C_bulk),\n",
    "    \"k--\",\n",
    "    label=\"Analytical\",\n",
    ")\n",
    "ax[1].set_xlabel(\"Distance from electrode / cm\")\n",
    "ax[1].set_ylabel(\"Concentration / mol cm$^{-3}$\")\n",
    "ax[1].set_title(\"Concentration profile\")\n",
    "ax[1].grid(alpha=0.3)\n",
    "ax[1].legend()\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. Compare the numerical solvers\n",
    "\n",
    "The same physical problem can be solved with the available Soft Potato solvers. Their differences are not about the physics; they are about the numerical approximation. In particular, the key issues are:\n",
    "\n",
    "- convergence: how the error decreases as the timestep is refined;\n",
    "- stability: whether the scheme can blow up or require restrictive time steps;\n",
    "- runtime: how much work is required for a given resolution.\n",
    "\n",
    "The main methods available in Soft Potato are:\n",
    "\n",
    "- `scipy_ivp` (adaptive method of lines): robust and flexible;\n",
    "- `crank_nicolson`: second-order in time and unconditionally stable for linear diffusion;\n",
    "- `implicit` (BTCS): first-order and unconditionally stable;\n",
    "- `explicit` (FTCS): first-order and conditionally stable via the CFL limit.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "solver_specs = {\n",
    "    \"SciPy IVP (Radau)\": (\n",
    "        \"scipy_ivp\",\n",
    "        {\"method\": \"Radau\", \"rtol\": 1e-7, \"atol\": 1e-10},\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    \"Crank-Nicolson\": (\"crank_nicolson\", {\"dt\": 0.01}),\n",
    "    \"Implicit BTCS\": (\"implicit\", {\"dt\": 0.01}),\n",
    "    \"Explicit FTCS\": (\"explicit\", {}),\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "results = {}\n",
    "timings = {}\n",
    "\n",
    "for label, (name, options) in solver_specs.items():\n",
    "    solver = get_solver(name, **options)\n",
    "    start = time.perf_counter()\n",
    "    results[label] = solver.solve(problem, t_span=(0.0, t_end), t_eval=t_eval)\n",
    "    timings[label] = time.perf_counter() - start\n",
    "    print(f\"{label:24s} solved in {timings[label]:.3f} s\")\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 5))\n",
    "for label, res in results.items():\n",
    "    current_num = n * F * A * res.fluxes[\"O\"]\n",
    "    ax.plot(\n",
    "        res.t[res.t > 0.0],\n",
    "        current_num[res.t > 0.0] * 1e6,\n",
    "        label=label,\n",
    "    )\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.plot(\n",
    "    result.t[positive],\n",
    "    current_exact * 1e6,\n",
    "    \"k--\",\n",
    "    lw=2,\n",
    "    label=\"Analytical Cotterell\",\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel(\"Time / s\")\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel(\"Current / µA\")\n",
    "ax.set_title(\"Chronoamperograms from implemented solvers\")\n",
    "ax.grid(alpha=0.3)\n",
    "ax.legend(fontsize=8)\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Error analysis against the analytical solution\n",
    "\n",
    "We now quantify the difference between the numerical and analytical current. The current is particularly sensitive to numerical error because it depends on the concentration gradient at the electrode surface.\n",
    "\n",
    "Define the absolute error:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "e_I(t) = \\left|I_{\\mathrm{num}}(t)-I_{\\mathrm{analytical}}(t)\\right|\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "and the maximum current error:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "L_\\infty = \\max_t e_I(t)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The root-mean-square error is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "RMSE = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_i \\left[I_{\\mathrm{num}}(t_i)-I_{\\mathrm{analytical}}(t_i)\\right]^2}\n",
    "$$\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "error_summary = {}\n",
    "\n",
    "for label, res in results.items():\n",
    "    current_num = n * F * A * res.fluxes[\"O\"]\n",
    "    times = res.t[res.t > 0.0]\n",
    "    current_vals = current_num[res.t > 0.0]\n",
    "    exact_vals = analytical_current(\n",
    "        times,\n",
    "        n=n,\n",
    "        F=F,\n",
    "        A=A,\n",
    "        D=D,\n",
    "        C_bulk=C_bulk,\n",
    "    )\n",
    "    error = np.abs(current_vals - exact_vals)\n",
    "    error_summary[label] = {\n",
    "        \"max_error\": np.max(error),\n",
    "        \"rmse\": np.sqrt(np.mean(error**2)),\n",
    "        \"relative_max\": np.max(error / np.abs(exact_vals)),\n    "    }\n",
    "    print(f\"{label:24s} max_error={np.max(error):.3e} A, rmse={np.sqrt(np.mean(error**2)):.3e} A\")\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 5))\n",
    "for label, res in results.items():\n",
    "    current_num = n * F * A * res.fluxes[\"O\"]\n",
    "    times = res.t[res.t > 0.0]\n",
    "    exact_vals = analytical_current(\n",
    "        times,\n",
    "        n=n,\n",
    "        F=F,\n",
    "        A=A,\n",
    "        D=D,\n",
    "        C_bulk=C_bulk,\n",
    "    )\n",
    "    relative_error = (current_num[res.t > 0.0] - exact_vals) / exact_vals\n",
    "    ax.plot(times, relative_error, label=label)\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.axhline(0.0, color=\"k\", linestyle=\"--\")\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel(\"Time / s\")\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel(r\"Relative current error, $(I-I_{exact})/I_{exact}$\")\n",
    "ax.set_title(\"Relative current error\")\n",
    "ax.grid(alpha=0.3)\n",
    "ax.legend(fontsize=8)\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 6. Stability and CFL condition\n",
    "\n",
    "The explicit solver uses a forward-time central-space (FTCS) scheme. Its stability is limited by the Courant-Friedrichs-Lewy condition:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\Delta t \\le \\frac{\\Delta x^2}{2D}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This means that the stable time step decreases quadratically with the spatial resolution. If the explicit solver takes a larger time step than this limit, it becomes unstable and loses physical meaning.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "dx = problem.dx\n",
    "dt_cfl = dx**2 / (2.0 * D)\n",
    "print(f\"dx = {dx:.3e} cm\")\n",
    "print(f\"Explicit CFL limit = {dt_cfl:.3e} s\")\n",
    "\n",
    "bad_dt = 1.1 * dt_cfl\n",
    "try:\n",
    "    unstable_solver = get_solver(\"explicit\", dt=bad_dt)\n",
    "    unstable_solver.solve(problem, t_span=(0.0, t_end), t_eval=t_eval)\n",
    "    print(\"This should not happen if stability checking works correctly.\")\n",
    "except ValueError as err:\n",
    "    print(\"The explicit solver rejected the time step:\")\n",
    "    print(err)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 7. Time-step convergence study\n",
    "\n",
    "A good model must converge as the time step is refined. We compare the current at a fixed time as the timestep changes.\n",
    "\n",
    "For a method with temporal error scaling as $e \\propto (\\Delta t)^p$, the slope of a log-log plot indicates the order of accuracy. Typical expectations are:\n",
    "\n",
    "- explicit FTCS: first-order;\n",
    "- implicit BTCS: first-order;\n",
    "- Crank-Nicolson: second-order;\n",
    "- adaptive `scipy_ivp`: controlled by tolerances rather than a fixed step.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "t_probe = 1.0\n",
    "dt_values = np.array([0.08, 0.04, 0.02, 0.01, 0.005])\n",
    "\n",
    "convergence_errors = {\n",
    "    \"Explicit FTCS\": [],\n",
    "    \"Implicit BTCS\": [],\n",
    \"    \"Crank-Nicolson\": [],\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "for dt in dt_values:\n",
    "    for label, solver_name in [\n",
    "        (\"Explicit FTCS\", \"explicit\"),\n",
    "        (\"Implicit BTCS\", \"implicit\"),\n",
    "        (\"Crank-Nicolson\", \"crank_nicolson\"),\n",
    "    ]:\n",
    "        try:\n",
    "            solver = get_solver(solver_name, dt=dt)\n",
    "            res = solver.solve(\n",
    "                problem,\n",
    "                t_span=(0.0, t_probe),\n",
    "                t_eval=np.array([0.0, t_probe]),\n",
    "            )\n",
    "            I_num = n * F * A * res.fluxes[\"O\"][-1]\n",
    "            I_exact = analytical_current(\n",
    "                t_probe,\n",
    "                n=n,\n",
    "                F=F,\n",
    "                A=A,\n",
    "                D=D,\n",
    "                C_bulk=C_bulk,\n",
    "            )\n",
    "            convergence_errors[label].append(abs(I_num - I_exact))\n",
    "        except ValueError:\n",
    "            convergence_errors[label].append(np.nan)\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 5))\n",
    "for label, errors in convergence_errors.items():\n",
    "    ax.loglog(dt_values, errors, \"o-\", label=label)\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.axvline(dt_cfl, color=\"k\", linestyle=\"--\", label=\"Explicit CFL limit\")\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel(r\"Time step, $\\Delta t$ / s\")\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel(\"Absolute current error / A\")\n",
    "ax.set_title(\"Time-step convergence study\")\n",
    "ax.invert_xaxis()\n",
    "ax.grid(True, which=\"both\", alpha=0.3)\n",
    "ax.legend()\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 8. Summary and main modelling lessons\n",
    "\n",
    "The diffusion-controlled chronoamperometry problem is physically described by:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\frac{\\partial C}{\\partial t}=D\\frac{\\partial^2 C}{\\partial x^2}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "with\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "C(x,0)=C^*, \\qquad C(0,t)=0, \\qquad C(L,t)=C^*\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The flux at the electrode is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "J(t)= -D\\left.\\frac{\\partial C}{\\partial x}\\right|_{x=0}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "and the current is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "I(t)= nFAJ(t)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "For a semi-infinite diffusion field, this becomes the Cotterell/Cottrell equation:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "I(t)=nFAC^*\\sqrt{\\frac{D}{\\pi t}}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The corresponding mass-transfer coefficient is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "k_m(t)=\\sqrt{\\frac{D}{\\pi t}}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This coefficient is time dependent because the diffusion layer grows over time. The current therefore decays as $t^{-1/2}$.\n",
    "\n",
    "Numerically, the solver choice matters:\n",
    "\n",
    "- the explicit scheme is restricted by the CFL limit;\n",
    "- implicit and Crank–Nicolson schemes are more forgiving for larger time steps;\n",
    "- adaptive ODE solvers are useful when stiffness is important;\n",
    "- the current is especially sensitive to numerical error because it depends on a derivative at the electrode surface;\n",
    "- a physically meaningful electrochemical solution must also be numerically converged.\n"
   ]
  }
 ],
